There are two gaps for engineers adopting AI: the one in front of you, involving frontier users running dozens to hundreds of agents, and the wider one behind you, involving companies whose developers have only opened Claude or Codex a few times.

Many companies are still very early in their agentic engineering journey, and the second gap is larger than it appears. If you're ahead, share what works; if you're behind, start small with one trusted loop instead of many agents.

@vasuman: "You’re already in the top 1% of AI users. Yes, there’s a gap between you and the folks on the frontier—the crazy kids running 20 terminals simultaneously with a knowledge base that rewrites itself."

Tip: Periodically run /doctor in Claude Code or ask Codex to audit for unused skills, MCPs, and context use. You've probably installed a few things that you forgot about or no longer need, and removing them will reduce token bloat.

@Swyx: "Occasional reminder to DELETE your skills. When you are bombarded constantly by 'this skill changed my life!! you have to try!!' on the timeline, you will pile up stuff that at best just eats context, and at worst interacts with other skills nastily in unforeseen ways if you don't stare at your traces."

The linked system, SmolForge, is a Git hosting platform with built-in CI/CD, site hosting, identity, and AI services, useful for managing coding agents.
